Meta commentary:
	This commentary is regarding how this document is structured since we have not formalized that yet.
	When referencing a named object in a specific context we will have to look up what perspective to take.
	For instance, here we may have some sort of "developer perspective" that is an implicit setting of documentation in a software project.

	In the language definition of Define Software Project we may have something like
		Set default documentation perspective: software planning

Define Software Project:

	Goal:

		Create a template processor that uses {mtl-1}.




Sketch: Definition of objectives
	Note: We should probably have a way to define a namespace we operate in so it becomes evident if we make a typo (rather than just matching some other parent object)

	Goal: Template Processor

		Pipeline: Defining a template

			Source: {Template} - A string containing the template
				Description: § Include Explanation for Template

			Process: Tokenize {Template} into {Tokenized Template}
			Process: Parse {Tokenized Template} into {Template AST}

			Final product: {Template AST}

		Pipeline: Applying a template

			Source: {Template AST}
			Source: {Template Context} - Context used for rending the template

			Intermediate: Intermediate Render
				Description: This is a text tree where a node can be a place holder or a node with a title that is a sequence consisting of placeholders and string literals.
				Motivation: By using this intermediate format we can use a common process for the final render, be that to a text tree or a text string.

			Process: Render {Template AST} into {Intermediate Render}
			Process: Render {Intermediate Render} into {Final Result}

			Final product: {Final Result}





Explanation for Template:

	Below is a typical template shown

	§ Show Examples/Template/T1

Language: Template Language


Attachments:
	Directory: Examples/Template
		Text Tree of Template Language: T1

			This is regular text
				§ Note: This is a template statement
					This belongs to the statement
					And so does this

				But this is part of the regular text
				We can of course use an «Inline Expression» in the template


Directory: Template
	Tokenizer: Main
		Literal Token:		«
			Enter Tokenizer: Inline

		Literal Token:		»
			Raise Exception

		Default:
			Emit Value as {Template Base Dir}/Token/Text

	Tokenizer: Inline
		Literal Token:		»
			Return

		Default:
			Emit Value as {Template Base Dir}/Token/Inline





Sketch: Tokenization

	Python
		#This is a specification for a tokenizer
		main = Tokenization_Specifier('main')
		inline = Tokenization_Specifier('inline')

		main.register_literal_token('«', A.Enter_Tokenizer(inline))
		main.register_literal_token('»', A.Raise_Exception)
		main.register_default(A.Emit(A.Wrap(Text)))

		inline.register_literal_token('»', A.Return)
		inline.register_default(A.Emit(A.Wrap(Inline)))

